systematicAndOtherDisciplines.body:
Theology (here meaning systematic theology) also connects with other fields of Christian study, such as exegesis, biblical theology, and ethics. My answer will combine elements of both a linear process (going in order from exegesis to biblical theology to systematic theology to ethics), as well as a circular process (going round and round between all of them several times). Imagine a ‘flow’ that runs through those four stages:

*(Figure 1.3, From exegesis to ethics, is in the Figures at the end of this chapter.)*

- **Exegesis:** understanding specific Bible passages;

- **Biblical Theology:** understanding how the whole Bible fits together;

- **Systematic Theology:** understanding what the Bible in its entirety says about any given topic;

- **Ethics:** knowing how we should live in response.

It makes sense to start with exegesis and end with ethics. Sometimes the flow will be linear, but not always. Suppose you want to study what the Bible says about divorce and remarriage. You might move from exegesis to biblical theology to systematic theology – and then realise that what you’ve discovered so far doesn’t all fit together well – so you realise you need to go back and do some more exegesis. This is perfectly normal and healthy.

Figure 1.3 also includes some auxiliary subjects, such as Greek, hermeneutics and philosophy. The list is not complete; it shows how other disciplines can help us become better reflective theologians.

*(Figure 1.4, a mind map, is in the Figures at the end of this chapter.)*
